<section class="hero hero--page wrap">
  <div class="hero-top">
    <nav class="breadcrumb reveal" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/en/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <a href="/en/resellers-capture-brand-traffic/">Resellers ahead of your brand</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <span>Afro cosmetics: who captures your traffic</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="hero-eyebrow reveal"><img width="918" height="832" src="/images/icon-orange.webp" alt="" /> Leak #3 · Wholesalers</span>
    <h1 class="reveal">Afro cosmetics SEO: why wholesalers own Google — <em>and why it's reversible</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub reveal">Search "shea butter for natural hair", "African black soap scrub" or "chebe powder". Look at who answers: wholesalers, marketplaces, reseller blogs. Rarely a brand.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:clamp(24px,4vw,48px)">
  <div class="wrap article reveal">
    <p class="lead">The afro cosmetics market has one striking anomaly on Google: <strong>the players with the legitimacy don't have the visibility, and vice versa</strong>. Here's the map of the terrain — and the crack in the generalists' position.</p>

    <figure>
      <img width="1080" height="864" src="/images/article-cosmetiques-karite.webp" alt="Jar of shea butter balm from an artisanal natural skincare brand" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>

    <h2>The market map: who captures what</h2>
    <p>Four families of players split your potential traffic:</p>
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Player</th><th>What they capture</th><th>Their strength</th><th>Their weakness</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Marketplaces</strong> (Amazon…)</td><td>Dry product queries</td><td>Crushing domain authority</td><td>Zero advice, zero culture</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Wholesalers &amp; multi-brand retailers</strong></td><td>Category + advice queries</td><td>Page volume, well-fed blogs</td><td>Generic content, no real product experience</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Beauty media &amp; blogs</strong></td><td>Advice and trend queries</td><td>Domain age, name recognition</td><td>They don't sell — and often recommend… your resellers</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Brands</strong> (you)</td><td>Too often: their own name only</td><td>Legitimacy, community, real expertise</td><td>Non-existent SEO infrastructure</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The reading is brutal but useful: the sector's traffic is captured by players none of whom have real experience of the product. That's an anomaly — and anomalies like this one end up correcting in favour of those who move early.</p>

    <h2>Wholesalers rank with generic content — that's exactly their weakness</h2>
    <p>Open the blog posts that dominate these queries. You'll find interchangeable texts, often churned out in bulk, written by people who have never used the products they describe. No lived experience, no nuance between textures, no understanding of the rituals.</p>
    <p>Yet Google increasingly weighs the real experience and embodied expertise behind a piece of content — and AI answer engines push that logic even further: they look for sources that <em>know</em>, not sources that <em>compile</em>.</p>
    <p>You formulate the products. You know hair porosity, textures, the mistakes people make, the questions your customers actually ask in your DMs. Every piece of content you publish at that level of precision is structurally out of a wholesaler's reach. They can copy your keywords; they can't copy your legitimacy.</p>
    <p>And that precise vocabulary — your community's — is exactly <a href="/en/seo-afro-haircare/">what the generalists miss</a>: "co-wash 4C", "castor oil for hair growth", the queries only a brand from the market knows to anticipate.</p>

    <h2>The queries where a brand beats a generalist every time</h2>
    <p>Without going into an exhaustive list — it depends on your catalogue and that's the Diagnostic's job — the winnable query families follow a clear pattern:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lived-in advice</strong>: queries where the answer demands having <em>lived</em> the subject (choosing by hair porosity, fixing a routine mistake). The generalist produces flat copy; you produce precise copy.</li>
      <li><strong>Product specificity</strong>: queries around the ingredients and uses you master better than anyone — origin, quality, processing.</li>
      <li><strong>Culture</strong>: queries where the cultural dimension is the answer. A wholesaler attempting it rings false instantly — and your audience senses it as much as Google does.</li>
      <li><strong>Your brand territory</strong>: your branded and semi-branded queries, which should already be won and often aren't (<a href="/en/brand-invisible-on-google/">check in 5 minutes</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What varies from one brand to the next: which of these queries carry volume <em>and</em> buying intent on <em>your</em> catalogue. That's where prioritisation happens.</p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>First, size the stakes: <a href="/en/lost-organic-revenue-calculator/">the lost organic revenue calculator</a> gives you the order of magnitude in 60 seconds.</p>
    <p>Then, if the number justifies acting: <a href="/en/seo-diagnostic/">the AfriRank Diagnostic</a> identifies your winnable queries, who captures them today, and the plan to take them back — quantified on your real data. €290 (~$310), credited toward your first month if you continue.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="kicker">Frequently asked questions</span><h2 class="sec-title">Your questions, my answers.</h2></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Isn't my Instagram content enough? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>It creates the demand, it doesn't capture it. Instagram doesn't rank on Google, and your posts vanish from the feed within 48 hours. The same knowledge, published on your site, works for you for years. It's not either/or: your social content proves, your site compounds.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Should I write about "type 4 hair" in general? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Not at first. Those broad queries are the most contested and the least likely to buy. The priority is the intersection between what you sell and what your customers type at the moment of choosing. The broad content comes later, to cement authority.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Can a wholesaler with 10 years of domain history be caught? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>On their core queries (dry product searches, big volumes), not quickly. On advice and specificity queries, yes — because their domain age doesn't make up for their lack of legitimacy, and Google's criteria are moving in your direction. You don't fight them everywhere: you pick the ground where they can't follow.</p></div></div>
    </div>
